  "live_records": {
    "q-36332": {
      "dom_id": "step-q36332",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "At which points on the curve $2xy+3{y}^{2}={x}^{2}+4$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy+3{y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+4 \\\\ 2xy+3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2xy+3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=4.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy+3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+6y)\\,i-(2y-2x)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ ((2x+6y)\\,i-(2y-2x)\\,j)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ 2y-2x & =0 \\\\ y & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x$ into the equation of our curve gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy+3{y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+4 \\\\ 2x(x)+3(x)^{2} & ={x}^{2}+4 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+3{x}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+4 \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $x=1$, we get $y=1$ and the corresponding point is $(1,1).$\n\n- If $x=-1$, we get $y=-1$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,-1).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
